    "body": "I suppose it would be more accurate to say that `stream.cancel()` behaves like `const reader = stream.getReader(); reader.cancel(); reader.releaseLock()`, where `reader` is a temporary reader. But that still wouldn't be a *good* explanation.\r\n\r\nIn essence, you use `stream.cancel()` to cancel a stream if you haven't locked it. If you have, you should use `reader.cancel()` instead, which requires that your `reader` still has the *active* lock on the stream.",
